    "narrative": "Today\u2019s offensive engine is anchored by **Vinnie Pasquantino** (76.21), whose matchup against Baz presents the day\u2019s premier value. **Vinnie Pasquantino** benefits from a massive 16% park factor and a 15% boost from the opposing SP skill set, cementing his role as our primary run-producer. He is closely followed by **Seiya Suzuki** (73.90), who takes advantage of a favorable park and a strong platoon edge against a lefty, and **Mickey Moniak** (69.47), who remains a high-upside play due to his dynamic platoon advantage and a slow-delivery pitcher that creates a prime environment for his speed-based skill set. **Corbin Carroll** (67.54) and **James Wood** (66.26) also provide essential high-floor production, with **James Wood** specifically benefiting from an elite .434 xwOBA profile that sustains his efficiency even against tougher competition.\n\nThe most difficult roster decision today was excluding **Kyle Karros** (46.70) from the active lineup. While he showed promise, he narrowly missed the cut because the optimizer favored the higher platoon and park-adjusted scoring of our middle infielders. Our bench remains disciplined; **Jose Caballero** (45.56) and **Josh Naylor** (45.03) both fell victim to unfavorable SP skill-matchups or platoon penalties, while **Edwin Arroyo** (39.82) was relegated to the bench to preserve game caps, falling below our 40-point efficiency floor. \n\nInvestors should note that we are currently monitoring several \"Lineup Pending\" statuses. **Gabriel Moreno**, **Dalton Rushing**, **Alex Freeland**, **Mickey Moniak**, and **Corbin Carroll** are locked into our projections based on historical slotting, but any drastic shift in their batting order could necessitate a recalibration of these efficiency scores. Furthermore, while **Brice Turang** (45.98) is active, his matchup against Skenes\u2014an elite arm with a low xERA\u2014presents a significant strikeout risk that keeps his score lower than his typical bas